# Build-context exclusions for Dockerfile.
#
# The repo had no .dockerignore at all, so every `docker build` uploaded the
# whole working tree to the daemon -- including a local `rust/target/`, which
# on a developer machine that has run `cargo build` is hundreds of MB to a few
# GB of intermediate artifacts that NO stage copies (the xray-builder stage
# compiles from source inside the image). Nothing below is consumed by any
# COPY in the Dockerfile; the five things the build actually needs are
# pyproject.toml, README.md, src/, plugins/ and rust/.
#
# Measured on one checkout with no rust/target/ present, the caches below
# alone were ~111MiB of a 212MiB tree (.mypy_cache 40MB, __pycache__ 69MiB
# across 191 directories, .pytest_cache 1.3MB, .ruff_cache 0.6MB); the build
# context shrank from 40.18MB to 212.13kB. With a local cargo build present,
# rust/target/ dwarfs all of it.

# Local Rust build output. `COPY rust/ /xray/rust/` would otherwise drag it in,
# and a host-built target/ in the image is both huge and wrong (built by a
# different toolchain, for a different glibc).
rust/target/

# Git metadata. The package version comes from src/code_indexer/__init__.py via
# [tool.hatch.version], NOT from VCS, so no build step needs git history.
# (In a git worktree .git is a FILE, not a directory -- this pattern matches
# both forms.)
.git
.gitignore
.gitattributes

# Python build/test detritus. Stale __pycache__ from the host would be copied
# into site-packages by `COPY src ./src` and shadow freshly-installed sources.
__pycache__/
**/__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
.pytest_cache/
.mypy_cache/
.ruff_cache/
.coverage
htmlcov/
build/
dist/
*.egg-info/
.venv/
venv/

# Never ship local developer state or scratch output into a build context.
.env
.idea/
.vscode/
